WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.801
AI can accelerate web application assessment, but it does not replace skilled testing.

00:00:06.801 --> 00:00:19.230
Its best uses are supportive: planning, organizing notes, clarifying architecture, summarizing evidence, explaining vulnerability classes, drafting report language, and helping analysts think through missing context.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:24.624
The assessor remains accountable for authorization, evidence, conclusions, and communication.

00:00:24.624 --> 00:00:27.673
Strong workflows keep scope clear.

00:00:27.673 --> 00:00:39.164
Authorized targets, accounts, roles, test windows, excluded systems, rate limits, data handling rules, communication paths, and escalation rules should guide every AI-assisted activity.

00:00:39.164 --> 00:00:47.372
AI suggestions should be checked against the rules of engagement before they influence testing or reporting.

00:00:47.372 --> 00:00:49.952
Evidence verification is central.

00:00:49.952 --> 00:01:03.788
Proxy logs, screenshots, scanner output, HTTP requests and responses, code snippets, API documentation, and behavior notes can all support findings, but they must be reviewed by a human.

00:01:03.788 --> 00:01:14.342
AI output should be treated as advisory, especially when it explains vulnerability classes, interprets code, summarizes tools, or drafts severity language.

00:01:14.342 --> 00:01:19.735
The goal is better authorized assessment quality, not uncontrolled automation.

00:01:19.735 --> 00:01:27.474
Protect sensitive data, minimize unnecessary exposure, preserve assumptions, and keep humans in the approval path.

00:01:27.474 --> 00:01:40.373
Used well, AI helps practitioners work faster and communicate more clearly while keeping professional judgment and safe conduct at the center of web application security assessment.
